  "Question": "If the radius of a circle is increased by \\(1\\) unit, the ratio of the new circumference to the new diameter is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\pi+2\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{2\\pi+1}{2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\pi\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{2\\pi-1}{2}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\pi-2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "For every circle, \\(\\pi\\) is the ratio of its circumference to its diameter. Increasing the radius produces another circle, so the same relationship holds:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{new circumference}}{\\text{new diameter}}=\\pi.\n\\]\nThe new diameter is positive, so this ratio is defined. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\pi}\\), choice \\(\\text{C}\\).",
